# Gordon Moore

Gordon Earle Moore (1929–2023) was an American engineer, chemist, and businessman who co-founded Intel Corporation and Fairchild Semiconductor. He is best known for formulating Moore's Law, an empirical observation and projection that the number of transistors on an integrated circuit doubles approximately every two years. After earning a doctorate in chemistry and physics from the California Institute of Technology, Moore joined the Shockley Semiconductor Laboratory. He subsequently left with a group of colleagues known as the "traitorous eight" to establish Fairchild Semiconductor in 1957, fundamentally shaping the Silicon Valley technology ecosystem. In 1968, alongside Robert Noyce, he established Intel, serving in various executive capacities including chief executive officer and chairman of the board. Under his leadership, Intel pioneered the commercialization of semiconductor memory and the microprocessor. In his later life, he established the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, directing billions of dollars toward scientific research, environmental conservation, and higher education.

## Early Life and Education

Gordon Earle Moore was born on January 3, 1929, in San Francisco, California, and raised in the nearby communities of Pescadero and Redwood City. Developing an early interest in chemistry through a neighbor's chemistry set, he decided to pursue the subject academically. He initially attended San Jose State University before transferring to the University of California, Berkeley, where he received a Bachelor of Science degree in chemistry in 1950.

He then enrolled at the California Institute of Technology (Caltech), earning a Ph.D. in chemistry and physics in 1954. Following his graduation, Moore conducted research on the physical chemistry of solid rocket propellants at the Applied Physics Laboratory at Johns Hopkins University in Maryland.

## Fairchild Semiconductor and the Traitorous Eight

In 1956, Moore returned to California to work at the Shockley Semiconductor Laboratory, a division of Beckman Instruments managed by Nobel laureate William Shockley. While the laboratory researched advanced silicon-based manufacturing methods, Shockley's management style prompted severe internal friction. In 1957, Moore and seven other researchers—dubbed the "traitorous eight" by Shockley—resigned to establish Fairchild Semiconductor, backed by Fairchild Camera and Instrument.

At Fairchild, Moore initially focused on developing diffusion processes and manufacturing techniques for transistor contacts. He eventually became the director of research and development in 1959. During his tenure, Fairchild made foundational advancements in semiconductor manufacturing, most notably the implementation of the planar process in 1960, which enabled the creation of the first commercially practical integrated circuits.

## Moore's Law

In 1965, while serving as Fairchild's director of research and development, Moore was asked by *Electronics* magazine to predict the future of the semiconductor industry. In his resulting paper, titled "Cramming more components onto integrated circuits," Moore observed a trend in the early production of microchips. He projected that the number of components (transistors) per integrated circuit for minimum cost would double approximately every year for the next ten years.

In 1975, at the IEEE International Electron Devices Meeting, he updated this projection to a doubling every two years, taking into account the increasing complexity of circuit design and limitations in photolithography. This observation, dubbed "Moore's Law" by Caltech professor Carver Mead, transitioned from a simple extrapolation to a guiding principle and self-fulfilling prophecy for the global electronics industry. It dictated the pace of semiconductor innovation, driving the exponential increase in computing power and the simultaneous reduction in component costs that enabled the digital revolution.

## Intel Corporation

In July 1968, Moore and Robert Noyce departed Fairchild Semiconductor to co-found Intel Corporation (a portmanteau of Integrated Electronics). Moore initially served as executive vice president before succeeding Noyce as president in 1975.

Under Moore's leadership, Intel shifted its focus from its early success in memory chips to the rapidly expanding market for microprocessors. Moore served as Intel's chief executive officer from 1975 to 1987, guiding the company through its critical growth phase as the personal computer industry emerged. He subsequently served as chairman of the board from 1979 to 1997, and was named chairman emeritus until his formal retirement in 2001.

## Philanthropy and Later Life

Beyond his corporate career, Moore was deeply involved in philanthropy and institutional leadership. He served as chairman of the board of trustees of Caltech from 1993 to 2000. In 2000, he and his wife Betty established the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, which became one of the largest private philanthropic foundations in the United States, focusing on environmental conservation, patient care, and scientific research.

Moore received numerous accolades for his technical and entrepreneurial achievements, including the National Medal of Technology in 1990 and the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2002. He died on March 24, 2023, at his home in Waimea, Hawaii, at the age of 94.

